<DocsLayout title="Architecture" description="Understand the Hologram Live kernel, transport, actor, module, and desktop boundaries.">
  <h1>Architecture</h1>
  <p>Hologram Live is a small product kernel with statically registered modules. It coordinates product concerns without absorbing module-owned business capabilities.</p>

  <h2>Kernel and modules</h2>
  <div class="doc-grid">
    <article class="doc-card"><h3>Kernel owns</h3><p>Configuration, lifecycle, routing, authentication, API serving, actors, telemetry, audit, and updates.</p></article>
    <article class="doc-card"><h3>Modules own</h3><p>Registry, files, <code>.holo</code> catalog behavior, conversation history, and node inventory.</p></article>
  </div>
  <p>Modules expose stable operation identifiers and declare dependencies. Startup resolves those dependencies before a module becomes available.</p>

  <h2>Application archives</h2>
  <p><a href={sitePath('/docs/holo-files')}><code>.holo</code> v4 files</a> separate their physical section table from a canonical application manifest containing ordered, κ-addressed layers. Readers retain v2/v3 compatibility. New archives add a verified application-directory projection so tools can inspect those relationships without changing the manifest's identity role.</p>

  <h2>Two protocol surfaces</h2>
  <p>Native clients use the versioned <code>hologram.live.v1.HologramLive</code> Protobuf/gRPC service. Browsers use JSON/HTTP routes described by Utoipa OpenAPI and rendered by Scalar.</p>
  <pre><code>CLI / desktop ── Protobuf + gRPC ──┐
                                   ├── hologram service ── modules
browser ─────── JSON + HTTP ───────┘</code></pre>

  <h2>Actors</h2>
  <p>Kameo provides process-local actors, bounded mailboxes, links, and supervision. A module lifecycle hook receives a narrow <code>ModuleContext</code> and may create an actor subtree when it owns long-lived mutable work. Request-driven modules remain stateless. Actor remoting is intentionally not the network API; cross-process work always crosses the authenticated gRPC boundary.</p>
  <p><code>.holo</code> execution uses a separate closed provider registry keyed by canonical <code>LayerKind</code>. A runtime-owned plan iteratively resolves the complete root and child κ closure before async providers prepare or start, with shared depth, application, layer, object, and byte limits across the tree. Admission proves each delegated grant attenuates its parent's effective grant and admits the child's own request before any provider prepares. Every evaluated relation crosses an awaited JSONL audit boundary carrying the real principal, non-secret identities, trusted source, and allow/deny outcome. The complete tree starts depth-first in manifest order and unload or rollback uses the exact reverse order. Each child provider receives only its admitted delegation. Only the root primary is invoked; child primaries share the parent's transactional lifetime. Wasmtime actors and the experimental Python OCI adapter remain private provider details, while shared status contains lifecycle state, aggregate tree resource counters, non-secret authorization evidence, and typed errors.</p>
  <p>Wasmtime's <code>core-wasm-v1</code> boundary imports nothing. The canonical layer entry selects a typed callable export inside the resolved module, and fixed <code>memory</code> and <code>holo_alloc</code> exports move bytes. Direct and resident providers validate the same entry before start and create a fresh instance per input. V1 reports byte-output completion, not a fabricated process exit code.</p>
  <p>Guest-contract selection remains separate from that callable entry. Source schema v4 maps a Wasm <code>contract</code> field to canonical <code>aux</code>. Empty <code>aux</code> normalizes to <code>hologram:guest/core-wasm@1</code>; explicit core-v1 and <code>hologram:guest/component@1</code> tags are accepted. Inspection and planning expose the normalized selector, while provider lookup uses the exact layer-kind and contract pair. Component archives reach only their dedicated direct or resident provider. The import-free WIT <code>run</code> boundary uses a fresh limited store per input while compiled code stays warm. Memory, fuel, input/output, deadline, and cancellation ceilings are enforced by a component-local engine without weakening core Wasm.</p>
  <p>Provider invocation keeps outputs separate from typed completion. Only the root primary supplies <code>returned</code> or a provider-observed <code>exited &#123; code &#125;</code>; child and non-primary layers cannot invent one. View, Tensor, and InferenceModel layers are non-exit-bearing. Direct completion is returned only after cleanup succeeds, while resident completion ends one invocation without unloading the application.</p>

  <h2>Routing and authority</h2>
  <p>The native client negotiates operation capabilities before choosing a local or remote target. Resources remain attached to the authority that created them, and mutations do not silently retry after an ambiguous transport failure.</p>

  <h2>Desktop</h2>
  <p>The Tauri application bundles <code>hologram</code> as a sidecar. The webview receives only narrow lifecycle and status commands—not a general-purpose shell. There is no server implementation under <code>src-tauri</code>.</p>

  <h2>Repository and deployment boundary</h2>
  <pre><code>src/                                  standalone server + CLI
crates/hologram-application-watch/    reusable watch/debounce engine
apps/desktop/src-tauri/               native adapter only
apps/desktop/src/                     webview frontend</code></pre>
  <p>The root <code>hologram-live</code> package is the deployable server product. <code>cargo build --release --locked --package hologram-live --bin hologram</code> builds it without compiling Tauri, WebKit, or the desktop frontend. Server release tags use that exact package and binary selection for Linux, macOS, and Windows artifacts.</p>
  <p>The application-watch engine is a small Tauri-independent workspace crate. It owns registration persistence, filesystem event filtering, debounce workers, and build state; a host injects paths, compile/import behavior, and change notification. The desktop adapter provides user-approved local paths and fixed sidecar commands. A future hosted development control plane can reuse the engine with a different storage/build adapter without importing desktop dependencies or receiving ambient access to a user's workstation.</p>

  <h2>Identity and permissions</h2>
  <p>The protocol already authenticates bearer credentials and authorizes stable operation IDs. The identity module will add signup, login, refresh, logout, principal, grant, and revocation operations. Fine-grained policy belongs in the shared authorizer at dispatch—not in individual HTTP handlers—so local and cloud enforcement cannot diverge.</p>
</DocsLayout>
